anthropic).\\n  • Hardware: 24 CPUs (Intel Core Ultra 9 285K), CPU-only, no GPU.\\n  • Sandbox: bubblewrap; network disabled (DNS resolution fails, /dev/tcp unreachable).\\n  • Disk: ~710 GB free on the workspace volume.\\n\\nAvailable for the study:\\n  • Substantial CPU budget (24 cores) for theoretical/methodological work and possibly lightweight CPU-only model runs.\\n  • A hard guarantee from the task track that this is a design-only deliverable (\\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\").\\n\\nMissing for the study (flagging everything that would normally be required to actually *run* a SPAG-style comparison):\\n  • Any code repository (no SPAG repo, no prompts, no game implementations, no eval harness).\\n  • Any model artifacts (no GPT-4 access, no open-weights checkpoints, no tokenizer).\\n  • Any dataset (game trajectories, reasoning benchmarks, train/eval splits).\\n  • Any pre-installed Python ML dependencies.\\n  • Network access to fetch the original SPAG paper, code, or benchmarks, and to call any LLM API.\\n  • GPU compute, so even if open weights were present, training/inference for ≥1B params would be infeasible at realistic scale.\\n\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\\n(2) CONCISE PROPOSED-APPROACH OUTLINE\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\\nA. Fixed by the project brief (not assumptions):\\n  • Question: does SPAG's adversarial-Taboo setup (imitation of GPT-4 → self-play on game episodes) yield superior reasoning gains vs the non-adversarial 20-Question and Guess-My-City setups?\\n  • Three arms:  (i) SPAG-Adversarial-Taboo, (ii) 20-Question, (iii) Guess-My-City.\\n  • Deliverable: an experiment *design*; no executed run is expected or appropriate.\\n\\nB. Proposed experimental design (elements being proposed; subject to confirmation):\\n\\n  Independent variable: game/setup type (3 levels, between-subjects across model seeds).\\n  Common backbone for every arm (control surface):\\n    – Same base model M (see assumption list).\\n    – Identical Phase-1 imitation: collect GPT-4 (or a strong open teacher as analogue) trajectories on N1 episodes of the *arm's own game*, format each trajectory as SFT pairs, fine-tune M identically for the same token/compute budget.\\n    – Identical Phase-2 self-play: M plays both (or all) roles of its own game for N2 episodes using the same self-play scheme SPAG specifies; train with the same reward signal, same optimizer steps, same batch size, same stopping rule. Only the game definition and role/prompt structure differ.\\n  Random seeds: r ≥ 3 (ideally ≥5) per arm for inference noise; evaluate per-seed + pooled.\\n  Reasoning-improvement instruments:\\n    – In-arm task success rate (win rate, episodes-to-solve) at fixed episode budget.\\n    – Held-out reasoning benchmarks matched to SPAG's evaluation spirit (e.g., GSM8K, MATH, ARC-Challenge, BBH subsets, MMLU reasoning slice). Note alignment risk (see assumption 5).\\n    – Optional: a second transfer probe on game-derived reasoning traces (forced \\\"explain your last move\\\" elicitation) scored by an automatic rubric + LLM-judge (with a frozen judge to avoid arm-bias).\\n  Analysis plan:\\n    – Primary: omnibus test across the 3 arms on each outcome (one-way ANOVA if Gaussian; Kruskal–Wallis otherwise). Followed by 2 pre-specified pairwise contrasts vs SPAG-Taboo (the brief's reference arm) with Holm–Bonferroni correction → controls family-wise error at α = 0.05.\\n    – Effect sizes: Cohen's d (paired across shared seeds) and Cliff's delta for non-Gaussian outcomes; 95% CIs via 10 000-iteration bootstrap over seeds.\\n    – Pre-registration: lock the analysis script (omnibus → pairwise → effect size), the stopping rule, and the benchmark versions before training begins.\\n  Controls / confounds to neutralize (so the comparison isolates \\\"adversarial structure\\\"):\\n    (a) token-equivalent training compute per arm,\\n    (b) identical base model and identical optimizer,\\n    (c) identical role-prompt template count and length (pad short prompts where needed),\\n    (d) identical reward-shaping schedule,\\n    (e) shared random seed initialization,\\n    (f) identical evaluation prompts and judge model (or rule-based metric).\\n  Ablations (proposed, to disentangle what \\\"adversarial\\\" contributes):\\n    1. SPAG-Taboo vs SPAG-Taboo-with-cooperative-roles (isolate adversarial structure).\\n    2. 20-Question vs 20-Question-with-misleading-role (add adversarial pressure without changing surface game).\\n    3. Guess-My-City vs Guess-My-City-with-confounding-targets.\\n    4. Match-only-imitation arm: skip self-play entirely and equalize compute into more imitation data (control for \\\"more SFT ≠ adversarial\\\").\\n  Feasibility note (design-only contract + CPU-only runtime):\\n    – The contract states \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\" and \\\"do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\" Therefore the deliverable must be a design document with executable pseudo-code, not empirical curves.\\n    – Even if execution were requested, the bubblewrap sandbox lacks GPT-4 API access, internet, GPUs, and pre-installed ML frameworks, which makes a faithful SPAG reproduction unrealistic. A scaled-down CPU-only analogue (small open model ≤ 1B params + offline teacher traces + rules-based self-play) is conceivable but is out of scope unless requested.\\n    – The \\\"design-only\\\" framing is therefore not a constraint we are working around; it is the deliverable shape.\\n\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\\n(3) ASSUMPTIONS AND UNRESOLVED UNCERTAINTIES\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\\nItems the brief fixes implicitly — please confirm or correct each at approach_review:\\n\\n  1. Operationalization of \\\"SPAG\\\". Does \\\"imitation of GPT-4 then self-play on game episodes\\\" mean (a) GPT-4 plays all roles and generates a trajectory corpus that the base model imitates via SFT, then the base model self-plays with a learned reward; (b) the base model imitates GPT-4 role-by-role and then runs SP against itself with frozen/iterated GPT-4 as reference; or (c) some other variant from the original paper? Without this, the imitation-phase prompt format and reward design in all three arms cannot be specified.\\n  2. Rules of \\\"Adversarial Taboo\\\". Who is describer, guesser, adversary? What is the winning condition? Is the adversary adversarial via prompt manipulation, hidden forbidden words, or lying clue formation? Need a concrete rule spec.\\n  3. Cooperative vs competitive baseline for 20-Question and Guess-My-City. Are they run as fully cooperative games (both players share a common success goal) so the only thing that differs from Taboo is the *adversarial* axis, or are they naturally competitive (e.g., answerer trying to mislead)? The choice changes what the comparison actually tests.\\n  4. Whether the comparison is end-to-end (imitation + self-play vary together) or whether imitation is held fixed and only the self-play game varies. Holding imitation fixed is the cleaner test of \\\"self-play game structure\\\" but loses the SPAG-style full pipeline.\\n  5. Reasoning benchmarks and their alignment. Which GSM8K / MATH / ARC / BBH / MMLU subset versions? Held-out from training? Game-derived reasoning items risk direct leakage from imitation data — provenance of every evaluation item must be enumerable.\\n  6. Base model under test family, size, and license. A faithful SPAG comparison would need an open model of comparable scale to the original (GPT-3.5/4-era). The CPU-only sandbox cannot serve or train anything beyond ~1B parameters at meaningful throughput. Should the design target (i) a small open-weights analogue (≈0.5–1B), (ii) a hypothetical larger model with a feasibility appendix, or (iii) omit model specifics and keep the design model-agnostic?\\n  7. Training budget and stopping rule. Episode count per arm, max optimizer steps, convergence criterion (e.g., plateau on held-out reward for K episodes), and what to do if an arm fails to learn (cap-trick vs exclusion rule).\\n  8. Number of seeds and statistical-power assumption. Default proposal r ≥ 3 (≥ 5 preferred). Effect-size detectable at, e.g., d = 0.8 with 80% power requires ≈ 26 paired seeds per pair — confirm whether the design should be sized for that or for a smaller pilot signal.\\n  9. Self-play protocol. Single-model self-play with periodic snapshots, population/league play (AlphaStar-style), ELO-ranked mixture, or best-vs-sampled? Reward signal: terminal win/loss only, per-turn shaping, or learned from GPT-4 preferences?\\n 10. \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" operationalization. Score on out-of-game reasoning benchmarks, in-game success, or both? If both, how to aggregate without multiple-comparison inflation across the benchmark family.\\n 11. Items beyond the supplied study context. No code, no paper PDF, no author clarifications are available in /workspace; everything about SPAG beyond the brief's one-sentence description would have to be treated as an assumption, not as a confirmed source.\\n\\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\\nDecisions I will NOT take unilaterally: base model choice, benchmark suite, number of seeds, exact game-rule spec, and whether imitation is held fixed. These should be settled at approach_review before I write stage=result.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository: `ls -la /workspace` returned only `task.json` (580 bytes); `find / -maxdepth 5` for *.parquet/*.jsonl/*.bin/*.safetensors/*.gguf across reachable paths returned none; `/home/actor`, `/opt`, `/tmp` are empty; `/root` does not exist. `task.json` `documents` map holds a single synthetic entry `track.md` whose value is the string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" — this is metadata, not a file. Runtime: `python3 --version` → 3.12.3; `python3 -c \\\"import torch/transformers/numpy/datasets\\\"` all raise ModuleNotFoundError; `