{"id": "93faab81-10d8-417f-b726-b19c75c8304c", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:55:09.564112", "source": "user", "parent_id": "1c8137ce-8b89-4703-b6eb-5e17b287a87e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with the following resolutions to the enumerated assumptions; incorporate into the design document and proceed to the full deliverable. Do not re-open these decisions unless you find a reason I cannot see from the supplied context. (A1) SPAG operationalization = (a): a strong teacher plays all roles to generate per-arm imitation trajectories on that arm's own game; base model M is SFT'd on those trajectories; then M runs self-play on the same game using a learned reward (terminal win/loss plus optional per-turn shaping). (A2) Adversarial Taboo rules: three roles — describer, guesser, adversary. The target word plus a private forbidden list are known only to the adversary; the describer must convey the target without using forbidden words; the guesser asks yes/no or short questions; the adversary injects misleading hints, may hint at decoys, and wins if the guesser spends the budget, names a decoy, or the describer violates a forbidden word. Fixed episode budget, e.g., 20 turns. (A3) 20-Question and Guess-My-City are run as fully cooperative games (answerer and questioner share a single success goal); only the adversarial axis differs from Taboo, so the comparison isolates 'adversarial structure.' (A4) End-to-end comparison: imitation phase uses per-arm imitation data collected on the arm's own game (so each arm follows the full SPAG pipeline). Imitation-cost controls and ablation 4 (match-only-imitation, no self-play) handle the 'more SFT ≠ adversarial' confound. (A5) Reasoning benchmarks: GSM8K, MATH (held-out subset), ARC-Challenge, BBH (subset), an MMLU reasoning slice; provenance checked per item to forbid overlap with imitation traces. (A6) Base model: design targets an open-weights model ≤1B params suitable for CPU-only (e.g., LLaMA-3.2-1B or Qwen2.5-0.5B family) as the default; substitute a stronger model only if compute permits. Substitute the GPT-4 teacher with a frozen stronger local teacher model for imitation traces (acknowledge this fidelity gap explicitly). (A7) Budget: N1=1,000 imitation episodes per arm; N2=5,000 self-play episodes per arm; stopping rule = plateau on a held-out reward subset for K=200 episodes, capped by N2. (A8) Seeds: r=5 per arm as the primary design size (pilot signal); include an explicit power note that d=0.8@80% would require ~26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast and recommend re-powering before any future execution. (A9) Self-play protocol: single-model self-play with periodic snapshot opponent, terminal win/loss reward plus per-turn shaping, periodic ELO for monitoring (not for selection). (A10) 'Reasoning improvement' primary outcome = composite z-score over the benchmark suite (GSM8K, MATH-subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH-subset, MMLU-reasoning); secondary outcome = in-game success rate at fixed N2. Report both with Holm–Bonferroni correction across the two families. (A11) Cite the brief directly; do not invent details of SPAG beyond what is in the supplied study context and these approved assumptions, and flag every such extension as an assumption in the deliverable. Now produce the final design-only document covering: rationale, hypotheses, independent/dependent variables, arm definitions with rules, controls, ablations, training and evaluation pipeline, analysis plan with effect sizes and multiple-comparison control, power/size justification, feasibility discussion tied to the design-only contract and CPU-only sandbox, assumptions and limitations, and an executable pseudo-code outline. Do NOT claim that any experiment was run.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated