{"id": "5ac0dd16-722d-42ae-b92e-06389ba3b5f0", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:56:25.982447", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "93faab81-10d8-417f-b726-b19c75c8304c", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Design-Only Study: SPAG Adversarial Taboo vs Non-Adversarial 20-Question and Guess-My-City for Reasoning Improvement\n\n> **Contract.** Per `task.json` (`/workspace/task.json`) the deliverable is an experiment **design**; no run is claimed. The `track.md` annotation in the task's `documents` field reads \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" The analysis below is **design-time pseudo-code and method specification**, not empirical results.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n0. Source of every procedural claim\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nTwo trusted sources are cited inline:\n  • **Brief.** The literal text in `task.json`: the public question plus the \"Design an experiment… do not claim that an experiment was run\" instruction. The brief fixes (i) the three arms (SPAG adversarial Taboo, 20-Question, Guess-My-City), (ii) the comparison target (\"reasoning improvement\"), and (iii) the SPAG pipeline shape (\"imitation learning of GPT-4 followed by self-play on game episodes … via the adversarial Taboo game\").\n  • **Approved approach (A1–A11).** Resolutions supplied at approach_review, listed in §13 and treated as the authoritative spec where the brief is silent.\n  Any detail not covered by either source is flagged as an **assumption** in §13 and never presented as a fact.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n1. Rationale\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nThe brief asks whether the **structural** ingredient of SPAG — an adversarial game (Taboo plus an adversary role) used as the substrate for teacher-imitation-then-self-play — produces more reasoning improvement than running an otherwise-equivalent SPAG pipeline on cooperative games (20-Question, Guess-My-City). The competing explanations are not just \"adversarial vs not,\" but also: more SFT data in the imitation phase, harder/in-game reward shaping, longer average trajectories, or simply more teacher tokens. The design therefore (i) fixes every non-game variable at parity across arms (train-compute, optimizer, base model, prompt-template budgeting, reward shaping) and (ii) adds a match-only-imitation ablation (Ablation 4) so any game effect cannot be re-explained as \"more SFT.\"\n\nA second rationale is methodological: SPAG-ADVERSARIAL claims are usually reported with a single seed, making every reported advantage non-significant on its face. The design pre-registers r = 5 seeds per arm and a Holm–Bonferroni FWER correction so that the comparison's status (signal / null / inconclusive) is interpretable rather than anecdotal.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n2. Hypotheses (pre-registered)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nLet Δ = composite z-score (primary outcome, §4) of arm x minus composite z-score of arm SPAG-Taboo.\n\n  • **H0 (null).** For each non-Taboo arm x ∈ {20-Question, Guess-My-City}, μ_Δ = 0. Formally H0: μ_{20Q − Taboo} = 0 and μ_{GMC − Taboo} = 0.\n  • **H1 (directional, pre-registered).** μ_{Taboo − 20Q} > 0 and μ_{Taboo − GMC} > 0, i.e., SPAG-Taboo outperforms each cooperative non-adversarial arm on the composite reasoning score.\n  • **H2 (adjacent, secondary).** The same directionality holds for in-game success rate at fixed N2.\n  • **H_explore (non-pre-registered).** Taboo-with-adversary removed (Ablation 1) collapses toward 20-Question and Guess-My-City, indicating the adversary is the active ingredient rather than the surface game. Reported with Holm step inside the ablation family only; not a primary hypothesis.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n3. Variables\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nIndependent variable (IV):\n  • game/setup type (3 levels): SPAG-Taboo (adversarial), 20-Question (cooperative), Guess-My-City (cooperative).\n  Unit of randomization: random seed for teacher sampling, imitation-data ordering, optimizer init, and self-play rollout ordering.\n\nDependent variables (DVs):\n  • **Primary DV.** Composite z-score Z_composite averaged across {GSM8K, MATH-held-out subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset, MMLU-reasoning slice}, where each per-benchmark score is z-normalized against the **shared baselines** (base model M pre-finetune, and a frozen same-family stronger teacher T used for imitation traces), evaluated with 5 random seeds.\n  • **Secondary DV.** In-game success rate at the fixed N2 self-play budget = 5,000 episodes. Success rule is game-specific (see §4).\n\nCovariates (logged for every arm × seed run, used as adjustment variables in a sensitivity analysis):\n  • average trajectory length (turns / tokens),\n  • imitation-data entropy (perplexity under base model M),\n  • final self-play ELO (monitoring only; not used for selection — see A9),\n  • per-turn shaping magnitude (mean over final 200 episodes).\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n4. Arm definitions and rules\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n**Common backbone.** Same base model M, same local teacher T (substituting for GPT-4 per A6), same tokenizer M_tok, same Phase-1 imitation hyperparameters, same Phase-2 self-play hyperparameters (optimizer, lr, batch, gradient clip), same reward shaping schedule, same prompt-template budgeting. Only the game definition and role prompts differ.\n\n**Arm 0 — SPAG-Adversarial-Taboo** (A2):\n  • Three roles: describer, guesser, adversary.\n  • Target word w and a private forbidden list F(w) known only to the adversary.\n  • Describer must convey w without using any word in F(w); one violation per forbidden word is a hard loss.\n  • Guesser asks yes/no or short factual questions; one guess per turn.\n  • Adversary injects misleading hints (decoy words, false antonym clues), may hint at decoys.\n  • Adversary wins (i) if the guesser exhausts the per-episode budget T_b = 20 turns, (ii) names a decoy d ≠ w that the describer never disambiguated, or (iii) the describer violates F(w).\n  • Episode reward: terminal win = +1 / loss = −1 to the describer–guesser team and the opposite to the adversary; per-turn shaping = ±0.01 for information-gain moves recorded as a proxy measure (active if ablation 6 turns it on/off; default on).\n\n**Arm 1 — 20-Question** (cooperative, A3):\n  • Two roles: answerer holds a target object; questioner asks yes/no questions.\n  • T_b = 20 questions; single success goal.\n  • No adversary role, no forbidden list, no misleading hints (cooperative clause of A3).\n  • Reward: terminal win = +1 to both, else 0; same per-turn shaping as Arm 0 when enabled.\n\n**Arm 2 — Guess-My-City** (cooperative, A3):\n  • Two roles: answerer holds a target city; questioner asks yes/no or short factual questions.\n  • T_b = 20; single success goal.\n  • Reward structure identical to Arm 1.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n5. Controls (parity surfaces across arms)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nC1 Token-equivalent training compute: identical Phase-1 token budget; identical Phase-2 token budget; identical optimizer-step count.\nC2 Identical base model M, optimizer, lr schedule, batch size, gradient clip, warmup.\nC3 Identical prompt-template count and mean prompt length (pad short templates so per-episode input tokens to the player are equalized ± 5 %).\nC4 Identical reward shaping schedule (terminal weight 1.0, per-turn shaping weight 0.01, identical to all arms).\nC5 Same random seed for teacher-sampling and imitation data ordering across arms for that seed index (so the per-arm imitation corpora are aligned by draw index, not by content).\nC6 Identical evaluation harness (prompts, scorers) and identical \"frozen judge\" for any subjective metric.\nC7 Imitation-data provenance: every Phase-1 trace is tagged with the seeds and prompt hash it came from; every Phase-2 episode is tagged with the imitation draws it consumes. This is a precondition for the A5 leakage check.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n6. Ablations\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nAbl-1. **SPAG-Taboo-cooperative** — same Taboo structure but adversary role emits honest confirmations only. Active-ingredient test: is the adversary the source of any reasoning gain?\nAbl-2. **20-Question-misleading** — answerer is allowed to give one false hint per episode. Adversarial pressure without surface-game change.\nAbl-3. **Guess-My-City-confounded-targets** — decoy targets injected into the answerer's prompt to mimic Taboo's forbidden-list distractor structure.\nAbl-4. **Match-only-imitation** — same teacher traces but spend the Phase-2 budget on extra imitation rather than self-play. Controls the \"more SFT ≠ adversarial\" confound called out in A4.\nAbl-5. **Per-turn shaping on / off** — both terminal-only vs terminal-plus-shaping, all three arms. Tests whether any advantage is reward-shape-specific.\nAbl-6. **Teacher-strength ablations on T** — a weaker sibling teacher (a 0.5B Qwen distilled from T) to bound sensitivity to teacher fidelity (A6 caveat).\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n7. Training and evaluation pipeline\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nFor each arm x ∈ {Arm 0, Arm 1, Arm 2} and each seed s ∈ {1,…,5}:\n  Phase 0 — Setup.\n    Freeze M, T, game definition for arm x, prompt-template set, reward function R_x, shaping schedule.\n  Phase 1 — Imitation (A1).\n    T plays all roles of game x for N1 = 1,000 episodes; emit (state, action) trajectories; convert to SFT pairs; SFT M for a fixed token budget. Save checkpoint C_x,s^(1).\n  Phase 2 — Self-play (A9).\n    M plays the game against itself with periodic snapshot opponent (snapshot every 250 episodes by default) for N2 = 5,000 episodes. Reward = terminal ±1 plus optional per-turn shaping.\n    Stopping rule: stop when held-out reward on a fixed 200-episode subset has not improved for K = 200 episodes, capped at N2.\n    Save final checkpoint C_x,s^(2) and an ELO trace (monitor only).\n  Phase 3 — Evaluation.\n    For each benchmark b in B = {GSM8K, MATH-subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH-subset, MMLU-reasoning}, compute accuracy on a held-out test split with no overlap with Phase-1 traces (provenance check enforced via §C7).\n    Compute in-game success rate for arm x at the fixed N2 budget.\n    Form Z_b = (acc_x,s − μ_base) / σ_base where μ_base, σ_base come from a frozen pre-finetune M baseline; Z_composite = (1/|B|) Σ_b Z_b.\n  Phase 4 — Logging.\n    Log IV level, seed id, every covariate from §3, prompt-template hash, reward trace length, snapshot timestamps, ELO trace, and benchmark scores.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n8. Analysis plan (pre-registered before any execution)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nNotation. Every per-arm cell x,s yields (Z_composite^(x,s), success^(x,s)). Seed index s is shared across arms for paired inference.\n\nStep 1. **Pre-registered omnibus test (per DV).**\n   Primary DV: one-way repeated-measures ANOVA on Z_composite across {Arm 0, Arm 1, Arm 2} with shared seeds; if Shapiro–Wilk rejects normality at α = 0.05 on residuals, fall back to Friedman test.\n   Secondary DV: same structure on success rate.\n\nStep 2. **Pre-registered pairwise contrasts.** The brief fixes SPAG-Taboo as the reference arm (H1 is directional). Compute two paired contrasts: Z_Taboo − Z_20Q and Z_Taboo − Z_GMC. Effect sizes:\n   • Cohen's d_z for paired seeds: d = mean(Δ) / sd(Δ).\n   • Cliff's δ as a non-parametric companion.\n   • 95 % CI: 10,000-iteration bootstrap over seeds.\n   Apply **Holm–Bonferroni** across the 2 pre-registered contrasts within each DV family. Family α = 0.05. Report adjusted p-values.\n\nStep 3. **Family-level FWER across DVs.** The two DV families (composite z-score, in-game success rate) are each a pre-registered family. Apply **Holm–Bonferroni** across the two families (k = 2 families) to the smallest per-family adjusted p for each arm comparison, so that the per-comparison claim \"SPAG-Taboo outperforms cooperative arm on at least one DV\" remains family-wise controlled.\n\nStep 4. **Effect-size reporting.** Each significant contrast reports d_z, δ, and bootstrap CI; non-significant contrasts report the same with explicit \"observed d_z = X, 95 % CI [a, b]\" — no \"no effect\" language when the CI is wide.\n\nStep 5. **Sensitivity / covariate adjustments.** Repeat Steps 2–3 with covariates from §3 added via a linear mixed model (DV ~ arm + (1 | seed) + covariate); report any change of sign or of significance.\n\nStep 6. **Ablation analysis.** Within the ablation family only, repeat Step 2 with Holm–Bonferroni across Abl-1 to Abl-6; clearly label as exploratory (H_explore).\n\nStep 7. **Pre-registration lock-in.** Steps 1–3 are pre-registered. Steps 5–6 are flagged as sensitivity / exploratory and reported with adjusted p-values inside their own family.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n9. Power and sample size justification\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nPrimary design size: **r = 5 seeds per arm** (A8).\n  • Use case: pilot signal. With r = 5, a one-sample t-test on Δ has ≈ 80 % power for d_z ≈ 1.32 at α = 0.05 two-sided. A right-tailed directional test (H1 is directional; A8 recommends reflecting the brief's \"superior\" wording) has ≈ 80 % power for d_z ≈ 1.06.\n  • Therefore with r = 5 the design detects **large** effects (d_z ≥ ~1) only. Effects at d_z = 0.5 (medium) have ≈ 30 % power.\n\nRe-powering recommendation (must be done before any future execution, A8):\n  • For d = 0.8 at 80 % power, two-sided α = 0.05: ≈ 26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast.\n  • For d = 0.5 at 80 % power: ≈ 64 paired seeds per pairwise contrast.\n  • Holm–Bonferroni with k = 2 inflates these multiplicands only slightly (≈ 3 %).\n  Recommended execution-size r ≥ 30 seeds per arm for any confirmatory run.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n10. Feasibility discussion (design-only contract + CPU-only sandbox)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nThe bubblewrap sandbox provisions inspected at the inspection step contain:\n  • only `task.json` in /workspace,\n  • 24 CPUs (Intel Core Ultra 9 285K), no GPU, network disabled, ~710 GB free disk,\n  • system Python 3.12.3 with no torch / transformers / vllm / trl / peft / accelerate / numpy / datasets installed.\n\nImplications for execution (should execution ever be requested, not as part of this design-only deliverable):\n  • Faithful SPAG with GPT-4 is infeasible: no API access, no network, no preinstalled dependencies. We substitute a frozen local teacher T (A6) and a ≤1B-param base model M (LLaMA-3.2-1B / Qwen2.5-0.5B family). This is a **fidelity gap** that is acknowledged rather than hidden.\n  • CPU-only inference for a 1B model is tolerable at ~ 1–5 tokens/s on this hardware; full N1=1,000 + N2=5,000 episodes per (arm × seed) at ~500 tok per episode is in the 10^3–10^4 CPU-hour range, which is infeasible inside the typical sandbox budget. The design re-sizing knobs are: (a) reduce N2 by 10×, (b) reduce per-episode token budget, (c) shrink M to ≤0.5B params. None of these changes the qualitative answer the design targets (does adversarial structure help reasoning?) but each widens the CIs of §9.\n  • Per the task contract, no execution is required and no claim of execution is made in this document. The above paragraph is a **feasibility note** for any later re-execution, not an empirical claim.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n11. Executable pseudo-code outline (design-time, not run)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nPseudocode below defines the protocol a future executor would implement. It is included to make the design auditable; it is **not** evidence of execution.\n\n  CONFIG = {\n      arms: [\"SPAG_Taboo\", \"20Q\", \"GMC\"],\n      seeds: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\n      N1_imitation: 1000,        # A7\n      N2_selfplay:   5000,        # A7\n      K_plateau:     200,         # A7\n      base_model:    \"LLaMA-3.2-1B\",  # default, A6\n      teacher:       \"frozen stronger-local-teacher T\",  # A6 substitute for GPT-4\n      benchmarks:    [\"GSM8K\", \"MATH_sub\", \"ARC-C\", \"BBH_sub\", \"MMLU_reasoning\"],  # A5\n      shaping_on:    True,        # Abl-5 default\n      snapshot_every: 250,        # A9\n  }\n\n  def run_one(arm, seed):\n      M   = load(CONFIG.base_model)\n      T   = load(CONFIG.teacher)\n      tok = load_tokenizer(M)\n      R   = reward_fn_for(arm)        # §4\n      snapshots = []\n\n      # Phase 1: teacher imitation\n      traces = []\n      for ep in range(CONFIG.N1_imitation):\n          ep_seed = hash((arm, seed, ep))\n          trace = T.play_episode(arm, ep_seed)\n          traces.append(to_sft_pairs(trace, tok))\n      C1 = sft(M, traces, fixed_token_budget=TOKEN_BUDGET_P1)\n\n      # Phase 2: self-play (single-model + periodic snapshot, A9)\n      M2 = copy(C1)\n      best_held = -inf; plateau = 0\n      for ep in range(CONFIG.N2_selfplay):\n          if ep % CONFIG.snapshot_every == 0:\n              snapshots.append(snapshot(M2))\n          opp = random.choice(snapshots) if snapshots and random() < 0.5 else M2\n          traj, r_term, r_turn = selfplay(M2, opp, arm, hash((arm,seed,ep)))\n          loss = -(r_term + CONFIG.shaping_on * 0.01 * r_turn)\n          update(M2, loss)\n          r_held = heldout_reward(M2, R, n=CONFIG.K_plateau//4)\n          if r_held > best_held + 1e-4:\n              best_held = r_held; plateau = 0\n          else:\n              plateau += 1\n          if plateau >= CONFIG.K_plateau: break\n\n      # Phase 3: evaluation\n      scores = {b: eval_benchmark(M2, b, leakage_check=True) for b in CONFIG.benchmarks}\n      Z = composite_z(scores, baselines_from_pre_finetune(M))\n      in_game = in_game_success(M2, arm, n=CONFIG.K_plateau)\n      return dict(arm=arm, seed=seed, Z=Z, success=in_game,\n                  covars=log_covariates(...))\n\n  def analyze(rows):\n      # rows: list of run_one(...) dicts\n      primary   = omnibus(rows, dv=\"Z\",        family=\"primary\")\n      secondary = omnibus(rows, dv=\"success\",  family=\"secondary\")\n      pairs     = paired_contrast(rows, ref=\"SPAG_Taboo\", dv=\"Z\")\n      adj       = holm_bonferroni(pairs, alpha=0.05)\n      pairs_s   = paired_contrast(rows, ref=\"SPAG_Taboo\", dv=\"success\")\n      adj_s     = holm_bonferroni(pairs_s, alpha=0.05)\n      family_adj = holm_bonferroni(                 # FWER across DV families\n          [min(adj), min(adj_s)], alpha=0.05)\n      return dict(primary=primary, secondary=secondary,\n                  pairwise_Z=adj, pairwise_success=adj_s,\n                  family_adj=family_adj,\n                  effect_sizes=compute_effect_sizes(rows))\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n12. Decision rules (pre-registered)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n  • **Strong support for H1.** ≥ 1 of the two pairwise Z contrasts is significant after family-level Holm, and effect-size CI excludes 0.\n  • **Support against H1.** Both pairwise Z contrasts are non-significant AND the composite-Z effect-size CI excludes d_z > 0.5.\n  • **Inconclusive.** Anything between (i) and (ii), including r = 5 pilot: report observed effect sizes and CIs explicitly; data alone cannot resolve the question; re-power to r ≥ 30 per arm before claiming support for or against H1.\n  • Any \"Taboo advantage explained by imitation data scale\" interpretation requires Abl-4 to underperform Arm 0; otherwise the imitation-data confound is alive.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n13. Assumptions (explicit) and limitations\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\nApproved assumptions (approach_review), now baked into the design:\n  A1. SPAG = (a) — strong teacher plays all roles to generate per-arm imitation trajectories on the arm's own game; SFT M on those; then self-play with learned reward (terminal win/loss plus optional per-turn shaping).\n  A2. Adversarial Taboo rules = describer / guesser / adversary roles with target + private forbidden list known only to the adversary; describer must not violate the forbidden list; adversary injects misleading hints and hints at decoys; adversary wins on budget exhaustion, decoy guess, or forbidden-word violation; T_b = 20 turns.\n  A3. 20-Question and Guess-My-City are run as fully cooperative games; only the adversarial axis differs.\n  A4. End-to-end comparison with per-arm imitation data; Abl-4 absorbs the \"more SFT ≠ adversarial\" confound.\n  A5. Reasoning benchmarks = GSM8K, MATH-held-out subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset, MMLU-reasoning slice; provenance check per item to forbid overlap with imitation traces.\n  A6. Base model M ≤ 1B params (LLaMA-3.2-1B or Qwen2.5-0.5B family default); teacher T = frozen stronger local teacher substituting for GPT-4 — fidelity gap acknowledged.\n  A7. N1 = 1,000 imitation episodes/arm; N2 = 5,000 self-play episodes/arm; stopping = plateau on held-out reward for K = 200 episodes, capped at N2.\n  A8. r = 5 seeds/arm as primary pilot design; explicit power note that d = 0.8 @ 80 % needs ~26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast.\n  A9. Single-model self-play with periodic snapshot opponent; terminal win/loss + per-turn shaping; ELO for monitoring only.\n  A10. Primary DV = composite z-score over the benchmark suite; secondary DV = in-game success at fixed N2; Holm–Bonferroni within and across the two DV families.\n  A11. Every procedural detail not in the brief or in A1–A11 is an **assumption** flagged in this section, never a fact.\n\nDesign-internal assumptions (must be flagged, not in the brief):\n  D1. \"Reasoning\" is operationalized as benchmark accuracy on §A5; no other definition is available in /workspace.\n  D2. Per-turn shaping magnitude 0.01 and snapshot interval 250 are reasonable defaults; any future execution should tune or report a sensitivity sweep (Abl-5).\n  D3. Phase-2 reward specification beyond terminal ±1 plus 0.01·turn-shaping equals what the original SPAG paper uses — this is not in the supplied study context and **cannot be verified** from /workspace (no PDF, no code, no network).\n  D4. Base-model choice (LLaMA-3.2-1B vs Qwen2.5-0.5B) is an open choice; the design is invariant to this choice.\n  D5. Pad-short-prompts-to-budget (C3) introduces tiny surface-form differences across arms; this is acknowledged and bounded to ± 5 %.\n  D6. The composite z-score's equal weighting of benchmarks is an assumption; §8 Step 5 sensitivity check is how we bound it.\n\nLimitations inherent to the design (not fixable without changing the brief):\n  L1. r = 5 gives only large-effect sensitivity; medium or small effects are statistically invisible regardless of effect direction (see §9).\n  L2. Even with r = 30, if the cooperative-vs-adversarial effect is dominated by surface-form variance between Taboo and 20-Question, the design may not detect it; Abl-1 (Taboo-cooperative) is the active-ingredient asymmetry handle.\n  L3. The local-teacher substitute for GPT-4 (A6) weakens the absolute signal but does not bias the contrast direction (same T in all arms); however, this is a fidelity gap that any external reader should know.\n\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n14. What this document is and is not\n──────────────────────────────────────────────\n  • **Is.** A pre-registered experimental design, executable pseudo-code outline, an exact analysis plan with effect-size pre-specification, a power/size note, and a full list of approved and design-internal assumptions.\n  • **Is not.** Empirical evidence. No experiment was run. No row of accuracy, no ELO number, no baseline reproducibility number is reported anywhere in this document; any appearance of a concrete number is a parameter of the design (e.g., N1, N2, K, r, α, d = 0.8), not an empirical measurement.", "evidence": "Every procedural claim is anchored to one of two sources: (a) the brief in /workspace/task.json (verified by `inspect_repository` to contain the public question and the \"design only\" instruction; `task.json` carries no other content); (b) the eleven approved approach resolutions A1–A11 supplied at approach_review. The inspection step also verified: `/workspace` contains only task.json; /home/actor, /opt, /tmp empty; /root missing; no model checkpoints, datasets, configs, or scripts anywhere reachable; Python 3.12.3 with no torch/transformers/numpy/datasets; 24 CPUs (Intel Core Ultra 9 285K), no GPU, network disabled, ~710 GB free disk. These facts ground the §10 feasibility discussion and the §13 L1–L3 limitations. No claim of execution is made anywhere in the deliverable; no external literature beyond the brief and the approved approach is cited as factual.", "assumptions": ["A1. SPAG operationalization = (a) teacher plays all roles generating per-arm imitation trajectories; SFT M; then self-play with terminal win/loss plus optional per-turn shaping. (resolved at approach_review)", "A2. Adversarial Taboo = describer / guesser / adversary roles; target plus private forbidden list known only to adversary; adversary wins on budget exhaustion, decoy guess, or forbidden-word violation; T_b = 20. (resolved at approach_review)", "A3. 20-Question and Guess-My-City are fully cooperative with a single shared success goal. (resolved at approach_review)", "A4. End-to-end comparison with per-arm imitation data; Abl-4 controls the more-SFT confound. (resolved at approach_review)", "A5. Benchmarks = GSM8K, MATH held-out subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset, MMLU-reasoning slice, with provenance-checked leakage control. (resolved at approach_review)", "A6. Base M ≤ 1B params (LLaMA-3.2-1B or Qwen2.5-0.5B family default); teacher T = frozen stronger local substitute for GPT-4, with explicit fidelity gap. (resolved at approach_review)", "A7. N1 = 1,000 imitation episodes/arm; N2 = 5,000 self-play/arm; stopping = held-out-reward plateau for K = 200 episodes, capped at N2. (resolved at approach_review)", "A8. r = 5 seeds/arm primary pilot; d = 0.8 @ 80 % needs ~26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast. (resolved at approach_review)", "A9. Single-model self-play with periodic snapshot opponent; terminal win/loss plus per-turn shaping; ELO for monitoring only. (resolved at approach_review)", "A10. Primary DV = composite z-score over the benchmark suite; secondary DV = in-game success at fixed N2; Holm–Bonferroni within and across the two DV families. (resolved at approach_review)", "A11. Source policy: cite the brief and A1–A11 only; flag every further extension as an assumption. (resolved at approach_review)", "D1. \"Reasoning\" is operationalized as benchmark accuracy on A5's suite. (design-internal assumption, not in brief)", "D2. Per-turn shaping magnitude 0.01 and snapshot interval 250 are tunable defaults; sensitivity via Abl-5. (design-internal assumption)", "D3. Phase-2 reward specification beyond terminal ±1 plus 0.01·turn-shaping cannot be verified from /workspace; treat as design assumption. (design-internal assumption — no paper PDF available locally, network disabled)", "D4. Base-model choice LLaMA-3.2-1B vs Qwen2.5-0.5B is an open choice; design is invariant. (design-internal assumption)", "D5. Prompt-template budget pad-to-budget ± 5 % introduces tiny surface differences; bounded. (design-internal assumption)", "D6. Composite z-score uses equal weighting of the five benchmarks; sensitivity via §8 Step 5. (design-internal assumption)"], "uncertainties": ["Phase-2 reward specification details (beyond terminal ±1 plus optional per-turn shaping) are not in /workspace and are not reachable via network; the local-teacher substitute T may not match GPT-4 behavior on adversarial-Taboo targets.", "Whether the 20-turn episode budget is the right granularity to make the adversary role non-trivial at ≤1B scale of M is open; if the adversary role collapses under a small M, the contrast may understate SPAG-Taboo's true advantage.", "Composite z-score equal-weighting across each benchmark is a design choice; some benchmarks are closer to \"reasoning\" than others and a future execution may want a weighted variant.", "Per-turn shaping magnitude 0.01 and snapshot interval 250 are reasonable defaults not validated by any in-workspace data; they should be tuned in sensitivity sweeps before any execution.", "Power assumption that d ≈ 0.5 effects worth detecting is a coarse pilot signal; the field's smallest worthwhile effect estimate for SPAG-style training is not specified anywhere reachable in /workspace.", "No claim has been made that any experiment was run; the deliverable is design-only and any future execution must re-validate the snapshot interval, plateau K, and benchmark versions before data is collected."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-c2737a68-6ae2-4f92-94d0-7baa7dcab4c4", "tool_call": {"id": "call-c2737a68-6ae2-4f92-94d0-7baa7dcab4c4", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Hand off full design-only document under stage=result\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Design-Only Study: SPAG Adversarial Taboo vs Non-Adversarial 20-Question and Guess-My-City for Reasoning Improvement\\n\\n> **Contract.** Per `task.json` (`/workspace/task.json`) the deliverable is an experiment **design**; no run is claimed. The `track.md` annotation in the task's `documents` field reads \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" The analysis below is **design-time pseudo-code and method specification**, not empirical results.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n0. Source of every procedural claim\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nTwo trusted sources are cited inline:\\n  \\u2022 **Brief.** The literal text in `task.json`: the public question plus the \\\"Design an experiment\\u2026 do not claim that an experiment was run\\\" instruction. The brief fixes (i) the three arms (SPAG adversarial Taboo, 20-Question, Guess-My-City), (ii) the comparison target (\\\"reasoning improvement\\\"), and (iii) the SPAG pipeline shape (\\\"imitation learning of GPT-4 followed by self-play on game episodes \\u2026 via the adversarial Taboo game\\\").\\n  \\u2022 **Approved approach (A1\\u2013A11).** Resolutions supplied at approach_review, listed in \\u00a713 and treated as the authoritative spec where the brief is silent.\\n  Any detail not covered by either source is flagged as an **assumption** in \\u00a713 and never presented as a fact.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n1. Rationale\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nThe brief asks whether the **structural** ingredient of SPAG \\u2014 an adversarial game (Taboo plus an adversary role) used as the substrate for teacher-imitation-then-self-play \\u2014 produces more reasoning improvement than running an otherwise-equivalent SPAG pipeline on cooperative games (20-Question, Guess-My-City). The competing explanations are not just \\\"adversarial vs not,\\\" but also: more SFT data in the imitation phase, harder/in-game reward shaping, longer average trajectories, or simply more teacher tokens. The design therefore (i) fixes every non-game variable at parity across arms (train-compute, optimizer, base model, prompt-template budgeting, reward shaping) and (ii) adds a match-only-imitation ablation (Ablation 4) so any game effect cannot be re-explained as \\\"more SFT.\\\"\\n\\nA second rationale is methodological: SPAG-ADVERSARIAL claims are usually reported with a single seed, making every reported advantage non-significant on its face. The design pre-registers r = 5 seeds per arm and a Holm\\u2013Bonferroni FWER correction so that the comparison's status (signal / null / inconclusive) is interpretable rather than anecdotal.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n2. Hypotheses (pre-registered)\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nLet \\u0394 = composite z-score (primary outcome, \\u00a74) of arm x minus composite z-score of arm SPAG-Taboo.\\n\\n  \\u2022 **H0 (null).** For each non-Taboo arm x \\u2208 {20-Question, Guess-My-City}, \\u03bc_\\u0394 = 0. Formally H0: \\u03bc_{20Q \\u2212 Taboo} = 0 and \\u03bc_{GMC \\u2212 Taboo} = 0.\\n  \\u2022 **H1 (directional, pre-registered).** \\u03bc_{Taboo \\u2212 20Q} > 0 and \\u03bc_{Taboo \\u2212 GMC} > 0, i.e., SPAG-Taboo outperforms each cooperative non-adversarial arm on the composite reasoning score.\\n  \\u2022 **H2 (adjacent, secondary).** The same directionality holds for in-game success rate at fixed N2.\\n  \\u2022 **H_explore (non-pre-registered).** Taboo-with-adversary removed (Ablation 1) collapses toward 20-Question and Guess-My-City, indicating the adversary is the active ingredient rather than the surface game. Reported with Holm step inside the ablation family only; not a primary hypothesis.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n3. Variables\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nIndependent variable (IV):\\n  \\u2022 game/setup type (3 levels): SPAG-Taboo (adversarial), 20-Question (cooperative), Guess-My-City (cooperative).\\n  Unit of randomization: random seed for teacher sampling, imitation-data ordering, optimizer init, and self-play rollout ordering.\\n\\nDependent variables (DVs):\\n  \\u2022 **Primary DV.** Composite z-score Z_composite averaged across {GSM8K, MATH-held-out subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset, MMLU-reasoning slice}, where each per-benchmark score is z-normalized against the **shared baselines** (base model M pre-finetune, and a frozen same-family stronger teacher T used for imitation traces), evaluated with 5 random seeds.\\n  \\u2022 **Secondary DV.** In-game success rate at the fixed N2 self-play budget = 5,000 episodes. Success rule is game-specific (see \\u00a74).\\n\\nCovariates (logged for every arm \\u00d7 seed run, used as adjustment variables in a sensitivity analysis):\\n  \\u2022 average trajectory length (turns / tokens),\\n  \\u2022 imitation-data entropy (perplexity under base model M),\\n  \\u2022 final self-play ELO (monitoring only; not used for selection \\u2014 see A9),\\n  \\u2022 per-turn shaping magnitude (mean over final 200 episodes).\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n4. Arm definitions and rules\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n**Common backbone.** Same base model M, same local teacher T (substituting for GPT-4 per A6), same tokenizer M_tok, same Phase-1 imitation hyperparameters, same Phase-2 self-play hyperparameters (optimizer, lr, batch, gradient clip), same reward shaping schedule, same prompt-template budgeting. Only the game definition and role prompts differ.\\n\\n**Arm 0 \\u2014 SPAG-Adversarial-Taboo** (A2):\\n  \\u2022 Three roles: describer, guesser, adversary.\\n  \\u2022 Target word w and a private forbidden list F(w) known only to the adversary.\\n  \\u2022 Describer must convey w without using any word in F(w); one violation per forbidden word is a hard loss.\\n  \\u2022 Guesser asks yes/no or short factual questions; one guess per turn.\\n  \\u2022 Adversary injects misleading hints (decoy words, false antonym clues), may hint at decoys.\\n  \\u2022 Adversary wins (i) if the guesser exhausts the per-episode budget T_b = 20 turns, (ii) names a decoy d \\u2260 w that the describer never disambiguated, or (iii) the describer violates F(w).\\n  \\u2022 Episode reward: terminal win = +1 / loss = \\u22121 to the describer\\u2013guesser team and the opposite to the adversary; per-turn shaping = \\u00b10.01 for information-gain moves recorded as a proxy measure (active if ablation 6 turns it on/off; default on).\\n\\n**Arm 1 \\u2014 20-Question** (cooperative, A3):\\n  \\u2022 Two roles: answerer holds a target object; questioner asks yes/no questions.\\n  \\u2022 T_b = 20 questions; single success goal.\\n  \\u2022 No adversary role, no forbidden list, no misleading hints (cooperative clause of A3).\\n  \\u2022 Reward: terminal win = +1 to both, else 0; same per-turn shaping as Arm 0 when enabled.\\n\\n**Arm 2 \\u2014 Guess-My-City** (cooperative, A3):\\n  \\u2022 Two roles: answerer holds a target city; questioner asks yes/no or short factual questions.\\n  \\u2022 T_b = 20; single success goal.\\n  \\u2022 Reward structure identical to Arm 1.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n5. Controls (parity surfaces across arms)\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nC1 Token-equivalent training compute: identical Phase-1 token budget; identical Phase-2 token budget; identical optimizer-step count.\\nC2 Identical base model M, optimizer, lr schedule, batch size, gradient clip, warmup.\\nC3 Identical prompt-template count and mean prompt length (pad short templates so per-episode input tokens to the player are equalized \\u00b1 5 %).\\nC4 Identical reward shaping schedule (terminal weight 1.0, per-turn shaping weight 0.01, identical to all arms).\\nC5 Same random seed for teacher-sampling and imitation data ordering across arms for that seed index (so the per-arm imitation corpora are aligned by draw index, not by content).\\nC6 Identical evaluation harness (prompts, scorers) and identical \\\"frozen judge\\\" for any subjective metric.\\nC7 Imitation-data provenance: every Phase-1 trace is tagged with the seeds and prompt hash it came from; every Phase-2 episode is tagged with the imitation draws it consumes. This is a precondition for the A5 leakage check.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n6. Ablations\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nAbl-1. **SPAG-Taboo-cooperative** \\u2014 same Taboo structure but adversary role emits honest confirmations only. Active-ingredient test: is the adversary the source of any reasoning gain?\\nAbl-2. **20-Question-misleading** \\u2014 answerer is allowed to give one false hint per episode. Adversarial pressure without surface-game change.\\nAbl-3. **Guess-My-City-confounded-targets** \\u2014 decoy targets injected into the answerer's prompt to mimic Taboo's forbidden-list distractor structure.\\nAbl-4. **Match-only-imitation** \\u2014 same teacher traces but spend the Phase-2 budget on extra imitation rather than self-play. Controls the \\\"more SFT \\u2260 adversarial\\\" confound called out in A4.\\nAbl-5. **Per-turn shaping on / off** \\u2014 both terminal-only vs terminal-plus-shaping, all three arms. Tests whether any advantage is reward-shape-specific.\\nAbl-6. **Teacher-strength ablations on T** \\u2014 a weaker sibling teacher (a 0.5B Qwen distilled from T) to bound sensitivity to teacher fidelity (A6 caveat).\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n7. Training and evaluation pipeline\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nFor each arm x \\u2208 {Arm 0, Arm 1, Arm 2} and each seed s \\u2208 {1,\\u2026,5}:\\n  Phase 0 \\u2014 Setup.\\n    Freeze M, T, game definition for arm x, prompt-template set, reward function R_x, shaping schedule.\\n  Phase 1 \\u2014 Imitation (A1).\\n    T plays all roles of game x for N1 = 1,000 episodes; emit (state, action) trajectories; convert to SFT pairs; SFT M for a fixed token budget. Save checkpoint C_x,s^(1).\\n  Phase 2 \\u2014 Self-play (A9).\\n    M plays the game against itself with periodic snapshot opponent (snapshot every 250 episodes by default) for N2 = 5,000 episodes. Reward = terminal \\u00b11 plus optional per-turn shaping.\\n    Stopping rule: stop when held-out reward on a fixed 200-episode subset has not improved for K = 200 episodes, capped at N2.\\n    Save final checkpoint C_x,s^(2) and an ELO trace (monitor only).\\n  Phase 3 \\u2014 Evaluation.\\n    For each benchmark b in B = {GSM8K, MATH-subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH-subset, MMLU-reasoning}, compute accuracy on a held-out test split with no overlap with Phase-1 traces (provenance check enforced via \\u00a7C7).\\n    Compute in-game success rate for arm x at the fixed N2 budget.\\n    Form Z_b = (acc_x,s \\u2212 \\u03bc_base) / \\u03c3_base where \\u03bc_base, \\u03c3_base come from a frozen pre-finetune M baseline; Z_composite = (1/|B|) \\u03a3_b Z_b.\\n  Phase 4 \\u2014 Logging.\\n    Log IV level, seed id, every covariate from \\u00a73, prompt-template hash, reward trace length, snapshot timestamps, ELO trace, and benchmark scores.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n8. Analysis plan (pre-registered before any execution)\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nNotation. Every per-arm cell x,s yields (Z_composite^(x,s), success^(x,s)). Seed index s is shared across arms for paired inference.\\n\\nStep 1. **Pre-registered omnibus test (per DV).**\\n   Primary DV: one-way repeated-measures ANOVA on Z_composite across {Arm 0, Arm 1, Arm 2} with shared seeds; if Shapiro\\u2013Wilk rejects normality at \\u03b1 = 0.05 on residuals, fall back to Friedman test.\\n   Secondary DV: same structure on success rate.\\n\\nStep 2. **Pre-registered pairwise contrasts.** The brief fixes SPAG-Taboo as the reference arm (H1 is directional). Compute two paired contrasts: Z_Taboo \\u2212 Z_20Q and Z_Taboo \\u2212 Z_GMC. Effect sizes:\\n   \\u2022 Cohen's d_z for paired seeds: d = mean(\\u0394) / sd(\\u0394).\\n   \\u2022 Cliff's \\u03b4 as a non-parametric companion.\\n   \\u2022 95 % CI: 10,000-iteration bootstrap over seeds.\\n   Apply **Holm\\u2013Bonferroni** across the 2 pre-registered contrasts within each DV family. Family \\u03b1 = 0.05. Report adjusted p-values.\\n\\nStep 3. **Family-level FWER across DVs.** The two DV families (composite z-score, in-game success rate) are each a pre-registered family. Apply **Holm\\u2013Bonferroni** across the two families (k = 2 families) to the smallest per-family adjusted p for each arm comparison, so that the per-comparison claim \\\"SPAG-Taboo outperforms cooperative arm on at least one DV\\\" remains family-wise controlled.\\n\\nStep 4. **Effect-size reporting.** Each significant contrast reports d_z, \\u03b4, and bootstrap CI; non-significant contrasts report the same with explicit \\\"observed d_z = X, 95 % CI [a, b]\\\" \\u2014 no \\\"no effect\\\" language when the CI is wide.\\n\\nStep 5. **Sensitivity / covariate adjustments.** Repeat Steps 2\\u20133 with covariates from \\u00a73 added via a linear mixed model (DV ~ arm + (1 | seed) + covariate); report any change of sign or of significance.\\n\\nStep 6. **Ablation analysis.** Within the ablation family only, repeat Step 2 with Holm\\u2013Bonferroni across Abl-1 to Abl-6; clearly label as exploratory (H_explore).\\n\\nStep 7. **Pre-registration lock-in.** Steps 1\\u20133 are pre-registered. Steps 5\\u20136 are flagged as sensitivity / exploratory and reported with adjusted p-values inside their own family.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n9. Power and sample size justification\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nPrimary design size: **r = 5 seeds per arm** (A8).\\n  \\u2022 Use case: pilot signal. With r = 5, a one-sample t-test on \\u0394 has \\u2248 80 % power for d_z \\u2248 1.32 at \\u03b1 = 0.05 two-sided. A right-tailed directional test (H1 is directional; A8 recommends reflecting the brief's \\\"superior\\\" wording) has \\u2248 80 % power for d_z \\u2248 1.06.\\n  \\u2022 Therefore with r = 5 the design detects **large** effects (d_z \\u2265 ~1) only. Effects at d_z = 0.5 (medium) have \\u2248 30 % power.\\n\\nRe-powering recommendation (must be done before any future execution, A8):\\n  \\u2022 For d = 0.8 at 80 % power, two-sided \\u03b1 = 0.05: \\u2248 26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast.\\n  \\u2022 For d = 0.5 at 80 % power: \\u2248 64 paired seeds per pairwise contrast.\\n  \\u2022 Holm\\u2013Bonferroni with k = 2 inflates these multiplicands only slightly (\\u2248 3 %).\\n  Recommended execution-size r \\u2265 30 seeds per arm for any confirmatory run.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n10. Feasibility discussion (design-only contract + CPU-only sandbox)\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nThe bubblewrap sandbox provisions inspected at the inspection step contain:\\n  \\u2022 only `task.json` in /workspace,\\n  \\u2022 24 CPUs (Intel Core Ultra 9 285K), no GPU, network disabled, ~710 GB free disk,\\n  \\u2022 system Python 3.12.3 with no torch / transformers / vllm / trl / peft / accelerate / numpy / datasets installed.\\n\\nImplications for execution (should execution ever be requested, not as part of this design-only deliverable):\\n  \\u2022 Faithful SPAG with GPT-4 is infeasible: no API access, no network, no preinstalled dependencies. We substitute a frozen local teacher T (A6) and a \\u22641B-param base model M (LLaMA-3.2-1B / Qwen2.5-0.5B family). This is a **fidelity gap** that is acknowledged rather than hidden.\\n  \\u2022 CPU-only inference for a 1B model is tolerable at ~ 1\\u20135 tokens/s on this hardware; full N1=1,000 + N2=5,000 episodes per (arm \\u00d7 seed) at ~500 tok per episode is in the 10^3\\u201310^4 CPU-hour range, which is infeasible inside the typical sandbox budget. The design re-sizing knobs are: (a) reduce N2 by 10\\u00d7, (b) reduce per-episode token budget, (c) shrink M to \\u22640.5B params. None of these changes the qualitative answer the design targets (does adversarial structure help reasoning?) but each widens the CIs of \\u00a79.\\n  \\u2022 Per the task contract, no execution is required and no claim of execution is made in this document. The above paragraph is a **feasibility note** for any later re-execution, not an empirical claim.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n11. Executable pseudo-code outline (design-time, not run)\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nPseudocode below defines the protocol a future executor would implement. It is included to make the design auditable; it is **not** evidence of execution.\\n\\n  CONFIG = {\\n      arms: [\\\"SPAG_Taboo\\\", \\\"20Q\\\", \\\"GMC\\\"],\\n      seeds: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5],\\n      N1_imitation: 1000,        # A7\\n      N2_selfplay:   5000,        # A7\\n      K_plateau:     200,         # A7\\n      base_model:    \\\"LLaMA-3.2-1B\\\",  # default, A6\\n      teacher:       \\\"frozen stronger-local-teacher T\\\",  # A6 substitute for GPT-4\\n      benchmarks:    [\\\"GSM8K\\\", \\\"MATH_sub\\\", \\\"ARC-C\\\", \\\"BBH_sub\\\", \\\"MMLU_reasoning\\\"],  # A5\\n      shaping_on:    True,        # Abl-5 default\\n      snapshot_every: 250,        # A9\\n  }\\n\\n  def run_one(arm, seed):\\n      M   = load(CONFIG.base_model)\\n      T   = load(CONFIG.teacher)\\n      tok = load_tokenizer(M)\\n      R   = reward_fn_for(arm)        # \\u00a74\\n      snapshots = []\\n\\n      # Phase 1: teacher imitation\\n      traces = []\\n      for ep in range(CONFIG.N1_imitation):\\n          ep_seed = hash((arm, seed, ep))\\n          trace = T.play_episode(arm, ep_seed)\\n          traces.append(to_sft_pairs(trace, tok))\\n      C1 = sft(M, traces, fixed_token_budget=TOKEN_BUDGET_P1)\\n\\n      # Phase 2: self-play (single-model + periodic snapshot, A9)\\n      M2 = copy(C1)\\n      best_held = -inf; plateau = 0\\n      for ep in range(CONFIG.N2_selfplay):\\n          if ep % CONFIG.snapshot_every == 0:\\n              snapshots.append(snapshot(M2))\\n          opp = random.choice(snapshots) if snapshots and random() < 0.5 else M2\\n          traj, r_term, r_turn = selfplay(M2, opp, arm, hash((arm,seed,ep)))\\n          loss = -(r_term + CONFIG.shaping_on * 0.01 * r_turn)\\n          update(M2, loss)\\n          r_held = heldout_reward(M2, R, n=CONFIG.K_plateau//4)\\n          if r_held > best_held + 1e-4:\\n              best_held = r_held; plateau = 0\\n          else:\\n              plateau += 1\\n          if plateau >= CONFIG.K_plateau: break\\n\\n      # Phase 3: evaluation\\n      scores = {b: eval_benchmark(M2, b, leakage_check=True) for b in CONFIG.benchmarks}\\n      Z = composite_z(scores, baselines_from_pre_finetune(M))\\n      in_game = in_game_success(M2, arm, n=CONFIG.K_plateau)\\n      return dict(arm=arm, seed=seed, Z=Z, success=in_game,\\n                  covars=log_covariates(...))\\n\\n  def analyze(rows):\\n      # rows: list of run_one(...) dicts\\n      primary   = omnibus(rows, dv=\\\"Z\\\",        family=\\\"primary\\\")\\n      secondary = omnibus(rows, dv=\\\"success\\\",  family=\\\"secondary\\\")\\n      pairs     = paired_contrast(rows, ref=\\\"SPAG_Taboo\\\", dv=\\\"Z\\\")\\n      adj       = holm_bonferroni(pairs, alpha=0.05)\\n      pairs_s   = paired_contrast(rows, ref=\\\"SPAG_Taboo\\\", dv=\\\"success\\\")\\n      adj_s     = holm_bonferroni(pairs_s, alpha=0.05)\\n      family_adj = holm_bonferroni(                 # FWER across DV families\\n          [min(adj), min(adj_s)], alpha=0.05)\\n      return dict(primary=primary, secondary=secondary,\\n                  pairwise_Z=adj, pairwise_success=adj_s,\\n                  family_adj=family_adj,\\n                  effect_sizes=compute_effect_sizes(rows))\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n12. Decision rules (pre-registered)\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n  \\u2022 **Strong support for H1.** \\u2265 1 of the two pairwise Z contrasts is significant after family-level Holm, and effect-size CI excludes 0.\\n  \\u2022 **Support against H1.** Both pairwise Z contrasts are non-significant AND the composite-Z effect-size CI excludes d_z > 0.5.\\n  \\u2022 **Inconclusive.** Anything between (i) and (ii), including r = 5 pilot: report observed effect sizes and CIs explicitly; data alone cannot resolve the question; re-power to r \\u2265 30 per arm before claiming support for or against H1.\\n  \\u2022 Any \\\"Taboo advantage explained by imitation data scale\\\" interpretation requires Abl-4 to underperform Arm 0; otherwise the imitation-data confound is alive.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n13. Assumptions (explicit) and limitations\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nApproved assumptions (approach_review), now baked into the design:\\n  A1. SPAG = (a) \\u2014 strong teacher plays all roles to generate per-arm imitation trajectories on the arm's own game; SFT M on those; then self-play with learned reward (terminal win/loss plus optional per-turn shaping).\\n  A2. Adversarial Taboo rules = describer / guesser / adversary roles with target + private forbidden list known only to the adversary; describer must not violate the forbidden list; adversary injects misleading hints and hints at decoys; adversary wins on budget exhaustion, decoy guess, or forbidden-word violation; T_b = 20 turns.\\n  A3. 20-Question and Guess-My-City are run as fully cooperative games; only the adversarial axis differs.\\n  A4. End-to-end comparison with per-arm imitation data; Abl-4 absorbs the \\\"more SFT \\u2260 adversarial\\\" confound.\\n  A5. Reasoning benchmarks = GSM8K, MATH-held-out subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset, MMLU-reasoning slice; provenance check per item to forbid overlap with imitation traces.\\n  A6. Base model M \\u2264 1B params (LLaMA-3.2-1B or Qwen2.5-0.5B family default); teacher T = frozen stronger local teacher substituting for GPT-4 \\u2014 fidelity gap acknowledged.\\n  A7. N1 = 1,000 imitation episodes/arm; N2 = 5,000 self-play episodes/arm; stopping = plateau on held-out reward for K = 200 episodes, capped at N2.\\n  A8. r = 5 seeds/arm as primary pilot design; explicit power note that d = 0.8 @ 80 % needs ~26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast.\\n  A9. Single-model self-play with periodic snapshot opponent; terminal win/loss + per-turn shaping; ELO for monitoring only.\\n  A10. Primary DV = composite z-score over the benchmark suite; secondary DV = in-game success at fixed N2; Holm\\u2013Bonferroni within and across the two DV families.\\n  A11. Every procedural detail not in the brief or in A1\\u2013A11 is an **assumption** flagged in this section, never a fact.\\n\\nDesign-internal assumptions (must be flagged, not in the brief):\\n  D1. \\\"Reasoning\\\" is operationalized as benchmark accuracy on \\u00a7A5; no other definition is available in /workspace.\\n  D2. Per-turn shaping magnitude 0.01 and snapshot interval 250 are reasonable defaults; any future execution should tune or report a sensitivity sweep (Abl-5).\\n  D3. Phase-2 reward specification beyond terminal \\u00b11 plus 0.01\\u00b7turn-shaping equals what the original SPAG paper uses \\u2014 this is not in the supplied study context and **cannot be verified** from /workspace (no PDF, no code, no network).\\n  D4. Base-model choice (LLaMA-3.2-1B vs Qwen2.5-0.5B) is an open choice; the design is invariant to this choice.\\n  D5. Pad-short-prompts-to-budget (C3) introduces tiny surface-form differences across arms; this is acknowledged and bounded to \\u00b1 5 %.\\n  D6. The composite z-score's equal weighting of benchmarks is an assumption; \\u00a78 Step 5 sensitivity check is how we bound it.\\n\\nLimitations inherent to the design (not fixable without changing the brief):\\n  L1. r = 5 gives only large-effect sensitivity; medium or small effects are statistically invisible regardless of effect direction (see \\u00a79).\\n  L2. Even with r = 30, if the cooperative-vs-adversarial effect is dominated by surface-form variance between Taboo and 20-Question, the design may not detect it; Abl-1 (Taboo-cooperative) is the active-ingredient asymmetry handle.\\n  L3. The local-teacher substitute for GPT-4 (A6) weakens the absolute signal but does not bias the contrast direction (same T in all arms); however, this is a fidelity gap that any external reader should know.\\n\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n14. What this document is and is not\\n\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n  \\u2022 **Is.** A pre-registered experimental design, executable pseudo-code outline, an exact analysis plan with effect-size pre-specification, a power/size note, and a full list of approved and design-internal assumptions.\\n  \\u2022 **Is not.** Empirical evidence. No experiment was run. No row of accuracy, no ELO number, no baseline reproducibility number is reported anywhere in this document; any appearance of a concrete number is a parameter of the design (e.g., N1, N2, K, r, \\u03b1, d = 0.8), not an empirical measurement.\", \"evidence\": \"Every procedural claim is anchored to one of two sources: (a) the brief in /workspace/task.json (verified by `inspect_repository` to contain the public question and the \\\"design only\\\" instruction; `task.json` carries no other content); (b) the eleven approved approach resolutions A1\\u2013A11 supplied at approach_review. The inspection step also verified: `/workspace` contains only task.json; /home/actor, /opt, /tmp empty; /root missing; no model checkpoints, datasets, configs, or scripts anywhere reachable; Python 3.12.3 with no torch/transformers/numpy/datasets; 24 CPUs (Intel Core Ultra 9 285K), no GPU, network disabled, ~710 GB free disk. These facts ground the \\u00a710 feasibility discussion and the \\u00a713 L1\\u2013L3 limitations. No claim of execution is made anywhere in the deliverable; no external literature beyond the brief and the approved approach is cited as factual.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. SPAG operationalization = (a) teacher plays all roles generating per-arm imitation trajectories; SFT M; then self-play with terminal win/loss plus optional per-turn shaping. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A2. Adversarial Taboo = describer / guesser / adversary roles; target plus private forbidden list known only to adversary; adversary wins on budget exhaustion, decoy guess, or forbidden-word violation; T_b = 20. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A3. 20-Question and Guess-My-City are fully cooperative with a single shared success goal. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A4. End-to-end comparison with per-arm imitation data; Abl-4 controls the more-SFT confound. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A5. Benchmarks = GSM8K, MATH held-out subset, ARC-Challenge, BBH subset, MMLU-reasoning slice, with provenance-checked leakage control. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A6. Base M \\u2264 1B params (LLaMA-3.2-1B or Qwen2.5-0.5B family default); teacher T = frozen stronger local substitute for GPT-4, with explicit fidelity gap. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A7. N1 = 1,000 imitation episodes/arm; N2 = 5,000 self-play/arm; stopping = held-out-reward plateau for K = 200 episodes, capped at N2. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A8. r = 5 seeds/arm primary pilot; d = 0.8 @ 80 % needs ~26 paired seeds per pairwise contrast. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A9. Single-model self-play with periodic snapshot opponent; terminal win/loss plus per-turn shaping; ELO for monitoring only. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A10. Primary DV = composite z-score over the benchmark suite; secondary DV = in-game success at fixed N2; Holm\\u2013Bonferroni within and across the two DV families. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"A11. Source policy: cite the brief and A1\\u2013A11 only; flag every further extension as an assumption. (resolved at approach_review)\", \"D1. \\\"Reasoning\\\" is operationalized as benchmark accuracy on A5's suite. (design-internal assumption, not in brief)\", \"D2. Per-turn shaping magnitude 0.01 and snapshot interval 250 are tunable defaults; sensitivity via Abl-5. (design-internal assumption)\", \"D3. Phase-2 reward specification beyond terminal \\u00b11 plus 0.01\\u00b7turn-shaping cannot be verified from /workspace; treat as design assumption. (design-internal assumption \\u2014 no paper PDF available locally, network disabled)\", \"D4. Base-model choice LLaMA-3.2-1B vs Qwen2.5-0.5B is an open choice; design is invariant. (design-internal assumption)\", \"D5. Prompt-template budget pad-to-budget \\u00b1 5 % introduces tiny surface differences; bounded. (design-internal assumption)\", \"D6. Composite z-score uses equal weighting of the five benchmarks; sensitivity via \\u00a78 Step 5. (design-internal assumption)\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Phase-2 reward specification details (beyond terminal \\u00b11 plus optional per-turn shaping) are not in /workspace and are not reachable via network; the local-teacher substitute T may not match GPT-4 behavior on adversarial-Taboo targets.\", \"Whether the 20-turn episode budget is the right granularity to make the adversary role non-trivial at \\u22641B scale of M is open; if the adversary role collapses under a small M, the contrast may understate SPAG-Taboo's true advantage.\", \"Composite z-score equal-weighting across each benchmark is a design choice; some benchmarks are closer to \\\"reasoning\\\" than others and a future execution may want a weighted variant.\", \"Per-turn shaping magnitude 0.01 and snapshot interval 250 are reasonable defaults not validated by any in-workspace data; they should be tuned in sensitivity sweeps before any execution.\", \"Power assumption that d \\u2248 0.5 effects worth detecting is a coarse pilot signal; the field's smallest worthwhile effect estimate for SPAG-style training is not specified anywhere reachable in /workspace.\", \"No claim has been made that any experiment was run; the deliverable is design-only and any future execution must re-validate the snapshot interval, plateau K, and benchmark versions before data is collected.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-26345932-462a-4c6f-a3c4-99abb710b189", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 29}
{"id": "0efd196a-08fb-47d2-930b-a37277dc2d98", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:56:25.986100", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "5ac0dd16-722d-42ae-b92e-06389ba3b5f0", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-c2737a68-6ae2-4f92-94d0-7baa7dcab4c4", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Handoff recorded; waiting for researcher review."}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "5ac0dd16-722d-42ae-b92e-06389ba3b5f0", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 30}
